      <section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Organize campaigns around customer intent</h2><p>Searches for termite inspection, rodent control, exterminators near me and recurring pest treatment may involve different urgency, offers and customer expectations. Group services when a different landing page, scheduling process or budget would be useful.</p><p>Keep the initial account structure manageable. Splitting a limited budget across too many campaigns can make performance difficult to evaluate.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Research keywords by service and geography</h2><p>Start with research themes such as pest control near me, termite inspection, rodent exterminator, ant treatment and commercial pest control. These are keyword ideas, not verified search volumes or cost-per-click figures.</p><p>Validate demand in Google Keyword Planner and review the real search terms from active campaigns. Location, seasonality and competition can change the opportunity significantly.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Watch for irrelevant searches</h2><p>Depending on the services offered, search traffic about DIY pest remedies, household products, exterminator jobs, pest-control licensing courses and free identification images may be poor fits for paid service campaigns.</p><p>Add negative keywords based on actual searches, not assumptions. Terms involving inspection, cost and treatment can sometimes indicate legitimate buyers, so avoid blocking them indiscriminately.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Write advertisements that reflect real services</h2><p>State the pest services offered, relevant territory and truthful customer next step. If an inspection is needed before a treatment proposal, describe it accurately.</p><p>Do not invent certifications, reviews, safety claims or guaranteed elimination outcomes. Environmental and pest-treatment claims should be consistent with the company&#x27;s actual practices and applicable requirements.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Send clicks to pest-specific landing pages</h2><p>A termite search should lead to a termite-focused service page. Rodent management searches should lead to content that explains the company&#x27;s relevant service and how to request help.</p><p>Keep pages fast on mobile with visible contact actions. Ask only for useful intake information and avoid implying that a marketing questionnaire replaces a professional inspection.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Track calls, inspections and qualified bookings</h2><p>Verify that conversion events correspond to real calls or submitted forms, and that each reaches the right team. Evaluate service territory, requested pest type and customer fit.</p><p>Where integrations allow, import confirmed inspection or service bookings and completed jobs into reporting. A calendar button click should never be counted as a booked pest-control appointment.</p></section>

